WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.431
For most of the last century, the inside
of Jupiter was educated guesswork.

00:00:05.711 --> 00:00:11.424
The diagrams looked tidy: a thick layer of
clouds, a deep ocean of hydrogen, and at

00:00:11.424 --> 00:00:14.728
the very centre, a compact ball of rock
and ice.

00:00:15.008 --> 00:00:20.703
Then, in 2016, a spacecraft arrived that
could actually measure the planet,

00:00:20.703 --> 00:00:26.474
skimming a few thousand kilometres above
the cloud tops on every close pass.

00:00:26.754 --> 00:00:31.099
What it found was messier, deeper, and
much stranger than the diagrams.

00:00:31.379 --> 00:00:36.479
These are five discoveries from NASA's
Juno mission, starting at the poles, where

00:00:36.479 --> 00:00:41.453
no spacecraft had ever had a good look,
and ending at a core that appears to be

00:00:41.453 --> 00:00:42.523
partly dissolved.

00:00:43.423 --> 00:00:49.423
Juno launched from Cape Canaveral on the
fifth of August, 2011, and spent five

00:00:49.423 --> 00:00:51.884
years crossing the solar system.

00:00:52.164 --> 00:00:58.948
On the fourth of July, 2016, it fired its
main engine and slipped into orbit around

00:00:58.948 --> 00:00:59.602
Jupiter.

00:00:59.882 --> 00:01:02.768
Two design choices made it unusual.

00:01:03.048 --> 00:01:05.344
First, it runs on sunlight.

00:01:05.624 --> 00:01:11.066
Out at Jupiter, sunlight is only about
four percent as strong as it is at Earth,

00:01:11.066 --> 00:01:15.760
so Juno carries three huge solar arrays,
each about nine metres long.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:21.835
Second, Jupiter surrounds itself with some
of the most intense radiation in the solar

00:01:21.835 --> 00:01:22.312
system.

00:01:22.592 --> 00:01:29.738
So Juno's most sensitive electronics sit
inside a titanium vault that weighs about

00:01:29.738 --> 00:01:30.958
180 kilograms.

00:01:31.238 --> 00:01:36.183
Instead of circling the equator, Juno
loops over the poles on long, stretched

00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:36.633
orbits.

00:01:36.913 --> 00:01:38.849
Each close pass is brief.

00:01:39.129 --> 00:01:43.501
Then the spacecraft swings far back out,
and waits weeks for the next one.

00:01:43.781 --> 00:01:47.121
At first, each of those orbits took 53
days.

00:01:48.022 --> 00:01:52.383
Discovery number one is at the poles, and
it looks almost designed.

00:01:52.663 --> 00:01:58.266
Over Jupiter's north pole, Juno's infrared
camera found one cyclone sitting at the

00:01:58.266 --> 00:02:03.527
centre, surrounded by a ring of eight
more, arranged roughly like an octagon.

00:02:03.807 --> 00:02:09.647
Over the south pole: one central cyclone,
surrounded by five, in a rough pentagon.

00:02:09.927 --> 00:02:14.758
Each of these storms is thousands of
kilometres across, and they are packed so

00:02:14.758 --> 00:02:17.111
tightly that their edges almost touch.

00:02:17.391 --> 00:02:21.128
On Earth, hurricanes drift toward the
poles and fall apart.

00:02:21.408 --> 00:02:23.850
On Jupiter, these storms stay put.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:27.848
Scientists watched the same pattern over
years of passes.

00:02:28.128 --> 00:02:32.331
The cyclones wobble around their
positions, but they do not merge.

00:02:32.611 --> 00:02:38.985
Then, in November 2019, a new and smaller
cyclone squeezed into the southern group.

00:02:39.265 --> 00:02:44.941
Nobody fully understands yet how such a
pattern forms, or why it stays so stable.

00:02:45.221 --> 00:02:47.417
That is still an open question.

00:02:48.317 --> 00:02:52.384
The second discovery: Jupiter's weather
does not stop at the clouds.

00:02:52.664 --> 00:02:58.103
The famous stripes are alternating bands
of wind, called belts and zones, blowing

00:02:58.103 --> 00:03:01.394
east and west at hundreds of kilometres
per hour.

00:03:01.674 --> 00:03:04.617
For decades, nobody knew how deep they
went.

00:03:04.897 --> 00:03:08.017
They could have been a thin skin, like
weather on Earth.

00:03:08.297 --> 00:03:10.711
Juno answered that with gravity.

00:03:10.991 --> 00:03:16.731
As the spacecraft swoops past, tiny tugs
on its radio signal reveal how mass is

00:03:16.731 --> 00:03:18.548
moving inside the planet.

00:03:18.828 --> 00:03:23.374
The answer: the jet streams reach about
3,000 kilometres down.

00:03:23.654 --> 00:03:28.274
Then there is the Great Red Spot, a storm
wider than our entire planet.

00:03:28.554 --> 00:03:34.828
Juno's microwave instrument showed its
roots extend more than 350 kilometres

00:03:34.828 --> 00:03:36.562
below the cloud tops.

00:03:36.842 --> 00:03:42.940
Gravity measurements from two close flybys
put the depth at around 500 kilometres.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:47.868
That is deep compared to any storm on
Earth, but shallow compared to the jets

00:03:47.868 --> 00:03:48.472
around it.

00:03:48.752 --> 00:03:54.153
Imagine a spinning coin, wide and thin,
floating on top of winds that go far

00:03:54.153 --> 00:03:54.650
deeper.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:59.761
Number three involves something
surprisingly basic: water.

00:04:00.041 --> 00:04:07.177
In 1995, NASA's Galileo probe plunged into
Jupiter's atmosphere and measured far less

00:04:07.177 --> 00:04:08.856
water than expected.

00:04:09.136 --> 00:04:13.986
It may simply have dropped into an
unusually dry spot, a bit like sampling

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:16.280
Earth's weather only over a desert.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.525
Juno measured from orbit instead, using
microwaves to see deeper into the

00:04:21.525 --> 00:04:23.498
atmosphere, near the equator.

00:04:23.778 --> 00:04:30.015
There, water makes up about 0.25 percent
of the molecules, almost three times the

00:04:30.015 --> 00:04:33.095
Sun's proportion of oxygen and hydrogen.

00:04:33.375 --> 00:04:34.955
Then came the lightning.

00:04:35.235 --> 00:04:40.534
Juno saw small flashes high up in the
atmosphere, in places where it should be

00:04:40.534 --> 00:04:42.300
too cold for liquid water.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.714
The proposed explanation is wonderfully
odd.

00:04:45.994 --> 00:04:52.100
Storms may throw ice crystals so high that
ammonia gas melts them into a slushy mix

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.645
of ammonia and water.

00:04:53.925 --> 00:04:58.715
Those slushy hailstones, nicknamed
mushballs, may grow heavy and fall deep

00:04:58.715 --> 00:05:02.534
into the planet, carrying ammonia and
water down with them.

00:05:02.814 --> 00:05:05.778
It is a model, not a direct observation.

00:05:06.058 --> 00:05:09.385
But it explains several puzzling
measurements at once.

00:05:10.285 --> 00:05:13.743
The fourth discovery goes all the way to
the centre.

00:05:14.023 --> 00:05:20.011
By tracking Juno's radio signal on every
close pass, scientists built up a detailed

00:05:20.011 --> 00:05:21.814
map of Jupiter's gravity.

00:05:22.094 --> 00:05:25.606
And gravity is shaped by where the mass
sits inside.

00:05:25.886 --> 00:05:30.377
The expected picture was a small, dense
core with a sharp boundary.

00:05:30.657 --> 00:05:35.868
Instead, the measurements point to a core
that is larger and partly dissolved, with

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:39.196
heavy elements mixed out into the hydrogen
around it.

00:05:39.476 --> 00:05:42.610
Scientists call it a dilute, or fuzzy,
core.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.273
How did it get that way?

00:05:44.553 --> 00:05:50.221
One idea, published in Nature in 2019, is
a head-on collision early in Jupiter's

00:05:50.221 --> 00:05:56.032
history, with a young planet around ten
times the mass of Earth, violent enough to

00:05:56.032 --> 00:05:59.291
shatter the original core and stir it
outward.

00:05:59.571 --> 00:06:04.414
Other researchers think the core could
have formed this way gradually, or been

00:06:04.414 --> 00:06:06.029
slowly eroded from within.

00:06:06.309 --> 00:06:08.381
The question is still open.

00:06:08.661 --> 00:06:13.249
What changed is that we now have
measurements to test the ideas against.

00:06:14.149 --> 00:06:17.646
And the fifth discovery is invisible, but
enormous.

00:06:17.926 --> 00:06:23.304
Jupiter's magnetic field is immense,
generated by a deep layer of hydrogen

00:06:23.304 --> 00:06:26.792
squeezed so hard it behaves like a liquid
metal.

00:06:27.072 --> 00:06:31.415
Juno found that the field is stronger and
stranger than expected.

00:06:31.695 --> 00:06:35.988
In some places, it is more than thirty
times as strong as Earth's.

00:06:36.268 --> 00:06:37.813
And it is lopsided.

00:06:38.093 --> 00:06:43.552
The northern hemisphere is a tangle of
intense patches, while near the equator

00:06:43.552 --> 00:06:49.081
sits an isolated hot spot of magnetic
field that scientists call the Great Blue

00:06:49.081 --> 00:06:49.431
Spot.

00:06:49.711 --> 00:06:54.943
By comparing Juno's map with measurements
from earlier missions, researchers found

00:06:54.943 --> 00:06:57.750
that the field is slowly changing over
time.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:03.135
Most of that change happens at the Great
Blue Spot, probably because deep winds are

00:07:03.135 --> 00:07:05.718
shearing the field and dragging it around.

00:07:05.998 --> 00:07:11.845
The same magnetic field powers Jupiter's
auroras, where Juno measured particle

00:07:11.845 --> 00:07:17.916
energies of up to 400,000 electron volts,
far beyond anything in Earth's northern

00:07:17.916 --> 00:07:18.441
lights.

00:07:19.341 --> 00:07:24.768
Juno was built to study Jupiter itself,
but in its later years it turned toward

00:07:24.768 --> 00:07:25.455
the moons.

00:07:25.735 --> 00:07:33.120
In June 2021, it passed within about 1,000
kilometres of Ganymede, the largest moon

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:34.899
in the solar system.

00:07:35.179 --> 00:07:41.533
In September 2022, it flew about 350
kilometres above the icy surface of

00:07:41.533 --> 00:07:47.093
Europa, a moon thought to hide a salty
ocean beneath its shell.

00:07:47.373 --> 00:07:55.860
Then, in December 2023 and February 2024,
it came within about 1,500 kilometres of

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.586
Io, the most volcanic world we know.

00:07:59.866 --> 00:08:05.831
Among the images was Loki Patera, a lava
lake roughly 200 kilometres across, with

00:08:05.831 --> 00:08:11.206
islands in the middle and a surface so
smooth in places that it reflected

00:08:11.206 --> 00:08:12.679
sunlight like glass.

00:08:12.959 --> 00:08:19.387
Each of those flybys also bent Juno's
path, shrinking its orbit from 53 days

00:08:19.387 --> 00:08:20.317
down to 33.

00:08:21.217 --> 00:08:26.651
Put it all together and Jupiter looks less
like a neat diagram and more like a living

00:08:26.651 --> 00:08:27.099
system.

00:08:27.379 --> 00:08:31.012
Storms locked into geometric patterns at
the poles.

00:08:31.292 --> 00:08:34.577
Winds reaching thousands of kilometres
down.

00:08:34.857 --> 00:08:36.735
Hail made of ammonia.

00:08:37.015 --> 00:08:40.023
A magnetic field that drifts over the
years.

00:08:40.303 --> 00:08:42.637
And a core that never quite settled.

00:08:42.917 --> 00:08:48.601
Juno's planned mission ran through
September 2025, and scientists are still

00:08:48.601 --> 00:08:51.178
working through what it sent home.

00:08:51.458 --> 00:08:56.723
If you want more space explained with real
images and real numbers, subscribe.

00:08:57.003 --> 00:09:00.490
Every source for this video is linked in
the description.
